{#
Renders one row in the message list.

Accepts either a MessageThread (`thread`) or a Message (`message`);
everything below normalises onto the same set of variables so the markup
is shared.

Draft handling: a row opens the compose dock instead of the reading pane
only when the row IS the draft — i.e. a thread holding a single draft
message, or a bare Message row flagged as a draft. A real conversation
carrying an unsent reply still opens the thread; that draft is edited from
inside the reading pane.

`draft_scope` (the Drafts list) overrides that: every row there is listed
because of its draft, so it opens that draft even when the thread also holds
the conversation it answers.
#}
{% set draft_scope = draft_scope|default(false) %}
{% set item = thread ?? message %}
{% set isThread = item.messages is defined %}

{#
Which account a row belongs to.

The unified lists merge every account, and the same message can genuinely be in
three of them at once — the same Message-ID delivered to three addresses puts
three rows in unified Trash, identical in sender, subject and minute, with
nothing on screen to say why there are three. Not a dedup bug; the rows are
real. They were just indistinguishable.

Shown only where it answers something: a list spanning accounts, on an install
that has more than one. A single-account install would get a dot that is the
same colour on every row forever, and an account-scoped list already says which
account it is at the top. `unified` defaults to true because most lists are —
the account view passes false.
#}
{% set unified = unified|default(true) %}
{% set rowAccount = item.account ?? null %}
{% set showAccountDot = unified and rowAccount is not null and accounts|length > 1 %}

{#
Is this conversation NEW — never yet put in front of the user?

Two sources, and the fallback is not defensive padding. A list render passes
`newThreadIds`: the set was read BEFORE the same request retired it, so the row
can still say "new" in the very frame that stops it being so — see
MailController::renderList(). Anything re-rendering one row on its own (the
turbo-streams under thread/status/, which redraw a row after a star or a
mark-read) has no such set and asks the thread directly, which by then answers
"already listed" — the badge goes when the row it belongs to is redrawn, rather
than being resurrected by whichever redraw happened last.

Threads only. A bare Message row is a message, and only a conversation arrives.
#}
{% set isNew = isThread
    and (newThreadIds is defined
        ? item.id in newThreadIds
        : newMail.isNew(item)) %}

{% if isThread %}
    {% set type = 'thread' %}
    {% set unread = item.unreadCount > 0 %}
    {% set subject = item.subject ?: ('thread_row.no_subject'|trans) %}
    {% set starred = item.starredAt is not null %}
    {% set date = item.lastMessageAt %}
    {% set count = item.messageCount %}

    {% set latest = item.messages|last %}
    {# Everyone who has written in the conversation, oldest first — not the
       newest sender, which made every thread you had answered look like it
       came from you. #}
    {% set sender = thread_participants(item)|join(', ') %}
    {# No |striptags: bodyText is ALREADY plain text — DraftPersister::plainTextBody()
       stripped the markup and then decoded the entities, and ingest does the same.
       Running it again does not remove markup, it removes the user's WRITING: a
       message whose text literally says `<b>bold</b>` was shown as "bold", and one
       containing `<img src=x ...>` had the whole thing silently deleted from the
       preview, so the list misreported what the message said. Twig's autoescaping
       is what makes this safe, and it is also what makes it correct — escaped, the
       angle brackets are shown; stripped, they were obeyed. #}
    {% set snippet = latest ? (latest.bodyText ?? '')|slice(0, 100) : '' %}

    {# `latest` comes from |last, which yields FALSE (not null) on an empty
       collection — so `is not null` passes and .isDraft blows up on a bool.
       A thread can legitimately end up with no messages (every message
       expunged provider-side), and this row is rendered for the whole inbox,
       so one such thread would 500 the entire list. #}
    {# Not `latest`: the collection is not ordered by date, so the draft is
       looked up by flag rather than assumed to be the last message. #}
    {% set draftMessage = item.messages|filter(m => m.isDraft)|last %}
    {% set draft = draftMessage
        and (draft_scope or item.messageCount == 1) %}
    {% set draftId = draft ? draftMessage.id : null %}

    {# In Drafts the who column asked the wrong question. Every draft is from
       you, so the participant list answered "ich" on every row and the column
       carried no information at all — the one thing that distinguishes one
       unsent message from another is who it is FOR. #}
    {# `?? []` is not defensive padding: a draft is saved the moment the body
       clears its minimum, which is routinely BEFORE a recipient has been typed,
       and toAddresses is null until one is. Without it every Drafts list holding
       one such draft is a 500 — which is exactly what the compose e2e spec
       found. #}
    {% if draft_scope and draftMessage %}
        {% set recipients = (draftMessage.toAddresses ?? [])
            |map(r => r.name ?: r.address)
            |filter(r => r is not empty) %}
        {% set sender = recipients|length > 0
            ? ('thread_row.to'|trans({ '%recipients%': recipients|join(', ') }))
            : ('thread_row.no_recipient'|trans) %}
    {% endif %}

    {# A draft on a hold. sentAt guards it for the reason the compose window
       guards it: once the mail has gone, submissionSendAt is the record of
       when it was due, not a schedule. #}
    {% set scheduledAt = (draftMessage and draftMessage.sentAt is null)
        ? draftMessage.submissionSendAt : null %}
    {# Which message the hold is on. Not draftId: that one is null unless the
       ROW opens the draft (a conversation carrying an unsent reply opens the
       thread), and the hold belongs to the draft either way. #}
    {% set scheduledId = scheduledAt is not null ? draftMessage.id : null %}

    {% set hasAttachment = item.attachmentCount > 0 %}
    {% set senderSeed = latest ? (latest.fromAddress ?: sender) : sender %}

    {% set href = path('app_mail_thread', { id: item.id }) %}
    {% set rowId = item.id %}
{% else %}
    {% set type = 'message' %}
    {% set unread = item.seenAt is null %}
    {% set subject = item.subject ?: ('thread_row.no_subject'|trans) %}
    {% set starred = item.starredAt is not null %}
    {% set date = item.receivedAt %}
    {% set count = 1 %}
    {% set sender = item.fromName ?: item.fromAddress %}
    {% set senderSeed = item.fromAddress ?: sender %}
    {# Already plain text — see the note on the thread branch above. #}
    {% set snippet = (item.bodyText ?? '')|slice(0, 100) %}

    {% set hasAttachment = item.hasAttachments %}

    {% set draft = item.isDraft %}
    {% set draftId = item.id %}
    {% set scheduledAt = (item.isDraft and item.sentAt is null)
        ? item.submissionSendAt : null %}
    {% set scheduledId = scheduledAt is not null ? item.id : null %}

    {% set href = path('app_mail_message', { id: item.id }) %}
    {% set rowId = item.id %}
{% endif %}

<li
    id="thread_{{ rowId }}"
    class="group relative hover:shadow-sm"
    data-controller="mail--message-row"
    data-mail--message-row-id-value="{{ rowId }}"
    data-unread="{{ unread ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
    {# Its own attribute beside data-unread rather than folded into it: the two
       are orthogonal, and a row that is `new="false" unread="true"` — scrolled
       past, never opened — is the case the whole feature exists to express. #}
    data-new="{{ isNew ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
    {# The id the client reports back once this row is actually in the document,
       so a list Turbo served from its prefetch cache still retires its badges —
       see mail--new-marker and MailController::markListed(). Only on rows that
       are claiming to be new; there is nothing to report about the others. #}
    {% if isNew %}data-new-id="{{ rowId }}"{% endif %}
    data-starred="{{ starred ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
>
    {# Full-row clickable overlay #}
<a
    {% if false == draft %}
        href="{{ href }}"
        data-action="click->mail--mail-pane#open"
    {% else %}
        href="{{ path('app_compose_edit', { id: draftId }) }}"
        data-turbo-frame="compose_dock"
    {% endif %}
    data-turbo-prefetch="false"
    class="pointer-events-auto absolute inset-0 z-0"
    aria-label="{{ subject }}"
    tabindex="-1"
    ></a>

    {# Which account this row arrived on: a small triangle tucked into the row's
       lower-left corner. A round dot beside the sender read as an unread
       marker — the very thing the list already signals with weight and the
       trailing dot — so the account moved out of the content entirely into a
       corner wedge, which is identity vocabulary, not status. The triangle is
       the account's own derived colour, clipped to the lower-left corner; the
       same everywhere it appears, only in a unified multi-account list. #}
    {% if showAccountDot %}
        {% set accountName = rowAccount.name ?: (rowAccount.email ?: rowAccount.username) %}
        {# `data-account-corner` is the whole of this row's part in making the
           corner optional: app.css sets its `display` from --list-corner-display,
           which AppearanceRenderer writes on <html> from the user's setting.
           No branch here, because the condition is not a fact about the row. #}
        <span data-account-corner
              class="pointer-events-none absolute bottom-0 left-0 h-2.5 w-2.5 {{ rowAccount.colorIndex|account_color }} [clip-path:polygon(0_0,0_100%,100%_100%)]"
              title="{{ 'thread_row.account'|trans({ '%account%': accountName }) }}"
              aria-hidden="true"></span>
    {% endif %}

    <div
        {# `list-row-y` for `py-2.5` and `row-unread` for the two inline unread
           alphas. Both resolve to exactly what those classes were — Comfortable
           density IS 0.625rem, and the tint keeps its 0.4 / 0.03 — so this is a
           move, not a restyle. What it buys is that both are now custom
           properties, which is what lets the density differ per surface and the
           unread tint scale with the emphasis setting. #}
        class="pointer-events-auto flex items-center @xl:items-center gap-3 pl-3 pr-4 list-row-y cursor-pointer transition-colors
            {{ unread ? 'row-unread' : 'bg-transparent' }}
            hover:bg-white/60 dark:hover:bg-white/[0.06]"
    >
        {# Avatar, doubling as the row's checkbox.
           The real input is still here and still named the same — the toolbar's
           select-all and every bulk action read [data-thread-select], and none
           of them should have to know the control became a circle. It is
           sr-only rather than removed so it stays focusable and keyboard
           users are not shut out; the avatar is its label, and `peer` drives
           the tick.

           Colour comes from the address and is never stored, so it is the same
           circle for the same person on every device — see
           SenderAvatarExtension. #}
        <label class="relative z-10 pointer-events-auto flex-shrink-0 cursor-pointer group/avatar">
            <input
                type="checkbox"
                data-action="change->mail--message-row#toggleSelect"
                data-thread-select value="{{ rowId }}"
                class="peer sr-only"
                aria-label="{{ 'thread_row.select'|trans({'%subject%': subject}) }}"
            >

            {# `data-row-avatar-face`: the hook the "sender discs off" setting
               paints a plain circle over. It cannot be hidden — see app.css —
               because this element's sibling input is the row's checkbox. #}
            <span data-row-avatar-face
                  class="flex items-center justify-center rounded-full
                         w-9 h-9 text-sm font-semibold select-none
                         transition-opacity
                         peer-checked:opacity-0 group-hover/avatar:opacity-0
                         {{ senderSeed|avatar_tone }}"
                  aria-hidden="true">{{ sender|avatar_initial(senderSeed) }}</span>

            <span class="absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-center rounded-full
                         bg-accent text-accent-ink
                         opacity-0 transition-opacity
                         peer-checked:opacity-100 group-hover/avatar:opacity-100
                         peer-focus-visible:ring-2 peer-focus-visible:ring-accent peer-focus-visible:ring-offset-1"
                  aria-hidden="true"><i class="fa-solid fa-check text-xs"></i></span>
        </label>

        {# Content — one line when the list is wide, three when it is not #}
        <div data-row-layout
             class="flex-1 min-w-0 flex flex-col @xl:flex-row @xl:items-center @xl:gap-4">

            {# Row 1 (stacked) / sender column (wide) #}
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between gap-2 @xl:justify-start @xl:w-40 @xl:flex-shrink-0">
                {# Sender and its marker travel together in one group, so the
                   badge sits to the LEFT of the name at both container widths.
                   Hanging it off the outer flex instead would have put it at
                   the far right of a stacked row (justify-between) and beside
                   the name when wide (justify-start) — the same marker in two
                   different places depending on how wide the list happens to
                   be. #}
                <span class="min-w-0 flex items-center gap-1.5">
                    {# The "New" marker: a filled accent pill carrying a WORD.
                       Deliberately nothing like the unread dot further down
                       this file, which is a bare 8px green circle — the two
                       mean different things ("never shown you" against "never
                       opened") and a user must not have to work out which
                       small round thing is which. A pill with text is a
                       different vocabulary from a dot, and the accent is the
                       token that follows whichever theme is on, so this reads
                       in light and dark without a second colour.

                       The visible word is aria-hidden and a fuller phrase sits
                       in sr-only beside it: "New" alone, announced in a list of
                       fifty rows, does not say new WHAT. #}
                    {% if isNew %}
                        <span class="shrink-0 inline-flex items-center rounded-full
                                     bg-accent text-accent-ink
                                     px-1.5 py-0.5 text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wide leading-none"
                              data-thread-new>
                            <span aria-hidden="true">{{ 'thread_row.new_badge'|trans }}</span>
                            <span class="sr-only">{{ 'thread_row.new'|trans }}</span>
                        </span>
                    {% endif %}

                    <span class="truncate text-sm {{ unread ? 'font-semibold text-ink' : 'font-normal text-ink-muted' }}">
                        {{ sender }}
                    </span>
                </span>
            </div>

            {# The rest of the row: subject, snippet, labels.

               Where the labels go differs by width, so the partial is included
               twice and each copy is shown at one of them. It renders no
               interactive element, so there is nothing to duplicate that a
               screen reader or a click could reach twice — the hidden copy is
               display:none and out of the tree entirely.

               Stacked they ride in front of the snippet, which is what the
               reference does and what reads well on three short lines. Wide,
               that same position wedges them between a subject and a preview
               that are already competing for one line, so they drop to a line
               of their own underneath. #}
            <div class="min-w-0 flex-1 flex flex-col">
                <div class="min-w-0 flex flex-col @xl:flex-row @xl:items-baseline @xl:gap-2">
                    <span class="truncate text-sm mt-1 @xl:mt-0 @xl:flex-shrink-0 @xl:max-w-[45%]
                                 {{ unread ? 'font-semibold text-ink' : 'font-normal text-ink-soft' }}">
                        {{ subject }}
                        {% if count > 1 %}
                            <span class="ml-1 text-xs font-medium text-ink-faint">{{ 'thread_row.message_count'|trans({'{ count }': count}) }}</span>
                        {% endif %}
                    </span>

                    {# A little air above the preview when stacked. It separates
                       the subject from the snippet, and the meta column mirrors
                       the same margin so the dot stays level with it. #}
                    <span class="min-w-0 flex-1 flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm mt-1.5 @xl:mt-0">
                        {# A draft with a send time on it. Without this the
                           Drafts list is the one place a scheduled message
                           lives and the only place that does not say so — the
                           toast has long faded, and the row is otherwise
                           indistinguishable from an unsent draft nobody
                           promised anything about.

                           The zone is named on `intl_date` because that filter
                           keeps whatever zone the value carries, and a Doctrine
                           timestamp carries UTC — so the unqualified call gets
                           the day wrong either side of midnight. `|date` takes
                           the environment's, which is already the user's, but
                           it is spelled out here so the two halves of one
                           timestamp cannot be read as coming from two clocks. #}
                        {% if scheduledAt is not null %}
                            {# Badge and cancel are one pill, not a badge with a
                               button parked next to it: the × means nothing on
                               its own, and reading it as "cancel THAT" depends
                               on it sharing the shape of the thing it undoes. #}
                            <span class="shrink-0 inline-flex items-center rounded-full
                                         text-[11px] font-medium bg-info/10 text-info whitespace-nowrap"
                                  data-scheduled-badge>
                                <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 pl-2 pr-1 py-0.5">
                                    <i class="fa-regular fa-clock" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                    {{ 'thread_row.scheduled'|trans({
                                        '%when%': 'compose.schedule.when'|trans({
                                            '%date%': scheduledAt|intl_date('dMMM', clock.timezone),
                                            '%time%': scheduledAt|date(clock.time, clock.timezone),
                                        }),
                                    }) }}
                                </span>

                                {# Calling the hold off from the row itself.
                                   Cancelling used to be one click further —
                                   open the draft, open the send menu — which
                                   is a long way to go to stop something you
                                   can already see.

                                   It lives ON TOP of the row's overlay anchor,
                                   the same way the star and the hover actions
                                   do, and by the same two means: `z-10` (this
                                   is a flex item, so z-index applies without a
                                   position) lifts it over the `z-0` anchor, so
                                   the anchor is never the click target; and
                                   mail--message-row#cancelSchedule stops the
                                   event before the row's own open handler sees
                                   it. No nested <form> — the post is a fetch
                                   answered with a Turbo Stream that redraws
                                   this row, exactly like archive and trash.

                                   Not hover-revealed, unlike those two: this
                                   is the only affordance for it and half the
                                   list has no hover at all. #}
                                <button type="button"
                                        data-action="click->mail--message-row#cancelSchedule"
                                        data-mail--message-row-cancel-schedule-url-param="{{ path('app_compose_mail_unschedule', {
                                            id:          scheduledId,
                                            type:        type,
                                            draft_scope: draft_scope ? 1 : 0,
                                        }) }}"
                                        data-cancel-schedule
                                        title="{{ 'thread_row.actions.cancel_schedule'|trans }}"
                                        aria-label="{{ 'thread_row.actions.cancel_schedule'|trans }}"
                                        {# `py-1` rather than stretching to the
                                           label: align-self on an inline-flex
                                           child gave an 11px tap target on a
                                           phone, which the mobile spec now
                                           measures rather than assumes. #}
                                        class="relative z-10 pointer-events-auto cursor-pointer
                                               flex items-center py-1 pl-1 pr-2 rounded-r-full
                                               text-info/80 hover:text-danger hover:bg-danger/10 transition-colors">
                                    <i class="fa-solid fa-xmark" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                                </button>
                            </span>
                        {% endif %}
                        <span class="@xl:hidden contents">
                            {% include '_partials/_thread_labels.html.twig' with { item: item } %}
                        </span>
                        {# A stacked row has one line for the preview, and a
                           scheduled draft has already spent most of it saying
                           when it goes — what is left of the snippet at 393px
                           is two characters and an ellipsis, which is noise
                           where the badge is information. Wide, both fit and
                           both are shown. #}
                        {# `@xl:inline-block`, not `@xl:block`, and this is the
                           second time this file has paid for it: Tailwind emits
                           `.hidden` AFTER `.block`, so `hidden @xl:block` is
                           hidden at every width — the container variant does
                           not outrank it. `.inline-block` is emitted after
                           `.hidden` and wins, and a flex item blockifies it
                           anyway, so `truncate` still truncates. The pairing
                           further down this file (`hidden @xl:flex`) works for
                           the same reason in the other direction. #}
                        {# `data-row-snippet` carries the preview-lines setting;
                           `truncate` stays for the one-line default, and
                           app.css releases its white-space only when the
                           setting asks for two. #}
                        <span data-row-snippet class="truncate text-ink-faint {{ scheduledAt is not null ? 'hidden @xl:inline-block' : '' }}">{{ snippet }}</span>
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="hidden @xl:flex items-center mt-1">
                    {% include '_partials/_thread_labels.html.twig' with { item: item } %}
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        {# Meta column. Stacked it is three rows — time, then the paperclip and
           the star, then the unread dot — mirroring the content's three text
           lines beside it. Wide the row is one line, so this is too.

           The hover actions still swap for the date, but only on the date's
           own row and only where there is a hover to speak of. They used to be
           absolutely positioned over the whole column, which is why nothing
           else could live here. #}
        {# The meta area takes the row's own shape. A stacked row is three
           lines tall and a vertical stack sits beside it; a wide row is one
           line, where the same stack has to spread out and leaves the date
           floating over a lone star with air between them. So: column when
           narrow, single line when wide. #}
        {# The meta area takes the row's own shape. Stacked, it stretches to the
           content's height and distributes, so the time sits on the sender's
           line, the icons on the subject's and the dot on the preview's —
           packing from the top instead left the third item hanging below the
           last line of text. Wide, the row is one line and so is this. #}
        <div data-row-meta
             class="flex flex-shrink-0 ml-2
                    flex-col items-end self-start
                    @xl:flex-row-reverse @xl:items-center @xl:justify-end @xl:gap-2
                    @xl:self-center @xl:w-36">

            <div class="relative flex items-center justify-end w-full h-5 @xl:h-6 @xl:w-auto @xl:flex-1">
                {# The paperclip rides with the date: wide, it sits just inboard
                   of it and vanishes with it when the hover actions take the
                   row over. Leaving it outside meant it stayed put underneath
                   them. Stacked there is no hover, so it simply sits there. #}
                <span class="absolute inset-0 flex items-center justify-end gap-1.5
                             text-xs text-ink-faint whitespace-nowrap
                             @xl:group-hover:invisible">
                    {# The visibility classes go on a wrapper, never on the <i>
                       itself: Font Awesome's own .fa-solid rule sets display,
                       and it lands after Tailwind's utilities, so `hidden` on
                       the icon is silently overridden and both copies render.
                       Every other icon in this file is wrapped for the same
                       reason. #}
                    {% if hasAttachment %}
                        <span class="hidden @xl:inline" aria-hidden="true">
                            <i class="fa-solid fa-paperclip text-[11px]"></i>
                        </span>
                    {% endif %}
                    {% if date %}
                        {% if date|date('Y-m-d') == 'now'|date('Y-m-d') %}
                            {{ date|date(clock.time) }}
                        {% elseif date|date('Y') == 'now'|date('Y') %}
                            {{ date|intl_date('dMMM') }}
                        {% else %}
                            {{ date|intl_date('dMMMy') }}
                        {% endif %}
                    {% endif %}
                </span>

                <div class="hidden @xl:flex absolute inset-0 items-center justify-end invisible group-hover:visible pointer-events-auto gap-1">
                {% if false == draft %}
                    <button type="button" data-action="click->mail--message-row#archive"
                            data-mail--message-row-archive-url-param="{{ path('app_status_archive', {'type': type, 'id': rowId}) }}"
                            aria-label="{{ 'thread_row.actions.archive'|trans }}"
                            title="{{ 'thread_row.actions.archive'|trans }}"
                            class="z-10 p-1.5 cursor-pointer rounded-lg text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 dark:hover:text-gray-200 hover:bg-gray-200 dark:hover:bg-gray-700 transition-colors">
                        <i class="fa-solid fa-box-archive w-4 h-4" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </button>
                {% endif %}

                <button type="button" data-action="click->mail--message-row#trash"
                        data-mail--message-row-trash-url-param="{{ path('app_status_trash', {'type': type, 'id': rowId}) }}"
                        aria-label="{{ 'thread_row.actions.delete'|trans }}"
                        title="{{ 'thread_row.actions.delete'|trans }}"
                        class="z-10 p-1.5 cursor-pointer rounded-lg text-gray-400 hover:text-red-600 hover:bg-gray-200 dark:hover:bg-gray-700 transition-colors">
                    <i class="fa-solid fa-trash w-4 h-4" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                </button>

                {% if false == draft %}
                    {# The button opens a menu rather than snoozing directly:
                       there is no defensible default wake time, and the one
                       this had (none at all) cleared the snooze instead of
                       setting it. #}
                    <div class="relative z-10" data-controller="ui--dropdown">
                        <button type="button"
                                data-action="click->ui--dropdown#toggle click->mail--message-row#stop"
                                aria-label="{{ 'thread_row.actions.snooze'|trans }}"
                                title="{{ 'thread_row.actions.snooze'|trans }}"
                                class="p-1.5 cursor-pointer rounded-lg text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 dark:hover:text-gray-200 hover:bg-gray-200 dark:hover:bg-gray-700 transition-colors">
                            <i class="fa-solid fa-clock w-4 h-4" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        </button>

                        {% include '_partials/_snooze_menu.html.twig' with {
                            controller: 'mail--message-row',
                            method:     'snooze',
                            url:        path('app_status_snooze', {'type': type, 'id': rowId}),
                            snoozed:    thread is defined and thread.snoozed|default(false),
                        } only %}
                    </div>

                    <button type="button" data-action="click->mail--message-row#markRead"
                            data-mail--message-row-read-url-param="{{ path('app_status_mark_read', {'type': type, 'id': rowId}) }}"
                            data-mail--message-row-read-param="{{ true == unread ? 'true' : 'false' }}"
                            aria-label="{{ unread ? 'thread_row.actions.mark_read'|trans : 'thread_row.actions.mark_unread'|trans }}"
                            title="{{ unread ? 'thread_row.actions.mark_read'|trans : 'thread_row.actions.mark_unread'|trans }}"
                            class="z-10 p-1.5 cursor-pointer rounded-lg text-gray-400 hover:text-gray-700 dark:hover:text-gray-200 hover:bg-gray-200 dark:hover:bg-gray-700 transition-colors">
                        {% if unread %}
                            <i class="fa-solid fa-envelope w-4 h-4" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        {% else %}
                            <i class="fa-solid fa-envelope-open w-4 h-4" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                        {% endif %}
                    </button>
                {% endif %}
            </div>
            </div>

            {# The star, on the row's trailing edge where the pointer already
               is. The paperclip lives with the date above — it is a fact about
               the row, not a control, and it belongs to the same line. #}
            <div class="flex items-center gap-2 h-5 mt-1 @xl:mt-0 @xl:order-first">
                {# Stacked, the paperclip belongs with the star: there is no
                   hover for it to vanish under, and a three-line row reads
                   better with its two marks together than with one stranded
                   beside the time. The wide copy lives with the date. #}
                {% if hasAttachment %}
                    <span class="@xl:hidden text-ink-faint" title="{{ 'thread_row.has_attachment'|trans }}">
                        <i class="fa-solid fa-paperclip text-xs" aria-hidden="true"></i>
                    </span>
                    <span class="sr-only">{{ 'thread_row.has_attachment'|trans }}</span>
                {% endif %}

                <button
                    type="button"
                    data-action="click->mail--message-row#toggleStar"
                    data-mail--message-row-star-url-param="{{ path('app_status_star', {'type': type, 'id': rowId}) }}"
                    class="z-10 pointer-events-auto cursor-pointer transition-colors
                           hover:text-amber-400
                           {{ starred
                              ? 'text-amber-400'
                              : 'text-ink-faint opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 focus-visible:opacity-100 max-md:opacity-100' }}"
                    aria-label="{{ starred ? 'thread_row.unstar'|trans : 'thread_row.star'|trans }}"
                >
                    <i class="fa-{{ starred ? 'solid' : 'regular' }} fa-star text-sm w-3.5 text-center"></i>
                </button>
            </div>

            {# Stacked only — a wide row already says "unread" twice over, with
               a bold sender and a bold subject.

               Boxed to the star's width rather than right-aligned on its own:
               the dot is 8px and the star glyph is 14, so aligning their right
               edges puts their centres 3px apart. #}
            {% if unread %}
                <span class="@xl:hidden h-5 mt-1.5 w-3.5 flex items-center justify-center shrink-0" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="w-2 h-2 rounded-full bg-success"></span>
                </span>
                {# No |default: a missing key comes back from |trans as the key
                   itself, which is a non-empty string, so the fallback could
                   never fire — it only hid that the key was missing, and a
                   screen reader read out "thread_row.unread". #}
                <span class="sr-only">{{ 'thread_row.unread'|trans }}</span>
            {% endif %}
        </div>

    </div>
</li>
